  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0031 database is a schema used for storing information related to an OAuth2 authorization server. It contains five tables: auth_user, oauth2_provider_accesstoken, oauth2_provider_application, oauth2_provider_grant, and oauth2_provider_refreshtoken.\nThe auth_user table contains information about the users of the authorization server, including their ID, password, last login, and other personal information. The table has a primary key on the ID column and allows null values for the last login column.\nThe oauth2_provider_accesstoken table contains information about the access tokens issued by the authorization server, including the token itself, the expiration date, the scope of the token, the application ID, the user ID, and the creation and update dates. The table has a primary key on the ID column and allows null values for the application ID and user ID columns.\nThe oauth2_provider_application table contains information about the applications registered with the authorization server, including their ID, client ID, redirect URIs, client type, authorization grant type, client secret, name, user ID, skip authorization, creation, and update dates. The table has a primary key on the ID column and allows null values for the user ID column.\nThe oauth2_provider_grant table contains information about the grants issued by the authorization server, including the code, expiration date, redirect URI, scope, application ID, user ID, creation, update, code challenge, and code challenge method. The table has a primary key on the ID column and allows null values for the application ID and user ID columns.\nThe oauth2_provider_refreshtoken table contains information about the refresh tokens issued by the authorization server, including the token, access token ID, application ID, user ID, creation, update, and revocation dates. The table has a primary key on the ID column and allows null values for the access token ID, application ID, and user ID columns.\nThe foreign keys in the schema ensure referential integrity, with the oauth2_provider_accesstoken table referencing the oauth2_provider_application table, the oauth2_provider_grant table referencing the oauth2_provider_application table, and the oauth2_provider_refreshtoken table referencing the oauth2_provider_application and oauth2_provider_accesstoken tables. The update and delete rules for the foreign keys are set to CASCADE, which means that if a referenced row is deleted or updated, the referencing row will also be deleted or updated.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing an OAuth2 client and server for Django. This system will provide a comprehensive solution, including endpoints, data handling, and logic, to integrate OAuth2 authentication into Django projects, enabling secure API access and token management.\n\n2) Objectives\n\n- Implement OAuth2 authentication to protect web APIs within Django applications.\n- Provide an OAuth2 authorization server for token management.\n- Ensure adherence to RFC standards using OAuthLib for compliance and security.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Integrating OAuth2 authentication into Django projects.\n- API Consumers: Accessing secured web APIs using OAuth2 tokens.\n- System Administrators: Managing OAuth2 clients, tokens, and user authentication.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Create multiple OAuth2 endpoints for authorization, token issuance, and token revocation.\n- Enable developers to manage applications (clients) with attributes like client ID, secret, and redirect URIs.\n- Handle OAuth2 grant types such as authorization code, implicit, and client credentials.\n- Allow users to authenticate and authorize third-party applications to access their data.\n- Utilize access and refresh tokens for session management, supporting token expiration and renewal.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Ensure that the system is RFC-compliant for standardization and security.\n- Provide a scalable solution capable of handling multiple simultaneous API requests.\n- Utilize secure methods for storing and transmitting sensitive information, such as client secrets and user credentials.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0031.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0031\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `personal_info` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `oauth2_provider_accesstoken` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expiration_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `application_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_accesstoken_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`application_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_application` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_accesstoken_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `oauth2_provider_application` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `client_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `redirect_uris` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `client_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `authorization_grant_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `client_secret` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `skip_authorization` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_application_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `oauth2_provider_grant` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `code` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expiration_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `redirect_uri` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `application_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `code_challenge` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `code_challenge_method` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_grant_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`application_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_application` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_grant_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `access_token_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `revocation_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`access_token_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_accesstoken` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`application_id`) REFERENCES `oauth2_provider_application` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth2_provider_refreshtoken_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
